    "case_summary": "A 55-year-old woman with a history of complete heart block (with permanent pacemaker implantation one year prior), hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and type 2 diabetes mellitus presented with worsening intermittent dyspnea over the past week. Associated symptoms included orthopnea, weight gain, intermittent chest pain, and lower extremity swelling.\n\nInitial examination revealed:- Tachypnea- Jugular venous distension- Diffuse pulmonary crackles- Lower extremity edema\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Brain natriuretic peptide: 1258 pg/mL- High-sensitivity troponin I: 62 ng/mL- Electrolytes: stableElectrocardiogram:- Wide complex tachycardia- Heart rate: 160-170 beats/minImage Title: Figure 1Image Description: Electrocardiogram showing a monomorphic wide QRS complex tachycardia consistent with ventricular tachycardiaEchocardiogram:- Dilated left ventricular cavity with LV wall hypertrophy- LV ejection fraction: 20-25%- Regional wall motion abnormalities in anterior, septal, and lateral walls- Moderate tricuspid and mitral regurgitation- Dyskinetic mid-septum- Significant LV desynchronyImage Title: Figure 2Image Description: Transthoracic echocardiogram: a parasternal long axis view showing a dilated left ventricle cavity with left ventricular wall hypertrophyCardiac MRI:- Patchy late gadolinium enhancement in multiple walls of the heartImage Title: Figure 3Image Description: Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging scan with late gadolinium enhancement in the inferior wall, basal wall, and mid-anterolateral wallCardiac Catheterization:- Elevated right and left heart-filling pressures- Low cardiac output- No significant coronary diseasePET scan:- Revealed cardiac granulomatous disease- No evidence of additional organ involvement",
